    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "KIE",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "Reversing an appellate court action, the New Jersey Supreme Court refused the nephew and guardian of Claire Conroy, an 84-year-old nursing home patient, permission to have her nasogastric feeding tube removed. The patient, a bed-ridden woman with irreversible physical and mental impairments, was judged to retain the right to make decisions about her own life-sustaining treatment, even though she was incompetent to exercise it. The court held that, in some circumstances, artificial life support measures could be withdrawn or withheld from institutionalized elderly patients with the involvement of the state ombudsman, but that here there was insufficient evidence that this patient would have refused feeding, or that termination of artificial feeding was in her best interests."
    }
